# {{.Name}}

A Caja **static-page** project — a small multi-page site. `main.caja` runs
once at build time and writes plain HTML/CSS into `dist/` via the `doc`
builtin module, the same way a static site generator's build step works.
Every page is built as a View tree rather than a hand-written HTML string;
see https://www.cajalang.com for more.

## Layout

```
main.caja        the site builder: lists the pages, wraps each in the shared
                 layout and navigation, writes dist/*.html and dist/style.css
pages/           one .caja file per page, each exporting `page`
  index.caja     the home page (a reference for every siriguela component)
  about.caja     a second page, so there is something to navigate to
assets/          images, documents, anything a page references
dist/            the generated site — never edit it by hand
```

Every file here imports two libraries, both with Caja's wildcard-import
feature, so every name is available bare rather than prefixed:

```caja
import * from "@caja/siriguela"   # the theme: card(), paragraph(), docSection()
import * from "@caja/ui"          # the structural layer: Page, definePage, column()
```

siriguela is the theme — the palette and the components that carry the look.
@caja/ui is what it is built on: the View tree, the site model (`Page`,
`definePage`, `navLinks`), the event handlers, and the CSS-in-Caja APIs. The
two export surfaces are disjoint, so importing both bare is collision-free.
Reach for a themed component first; drop to @caja/ui for anything structural
the theme doesn't wrap.

**Adding a page:** create `pages/name.caja` exporting
`let page = definePage(title: "Title", path: "name.html", content: content)`,
import it in
`main.caja` (`import "pages/name" as name`) and append `name.page` to the
`site` list. The navigation, the layout and the writer all pick it up from
that one list. A page module only *builds* its page; writing files is
`main.caja`'s job — which is also why `caja run -f pages/name.caja` still
works for a quick check while `caja run -f main.caja` is refused.

**Assets:** put files in `assets/` and reference them as `assets/<name>` from
a page (`image("assets/logo.svg", "Logo")`). Every build recreates
`dist/assets/` from scratch, so a renamed or deleted asset disappears from
the output too. Generated pages are *not* cleared: if you rename a page,
delete its old `dist/*.html` yourself (or delete `dist/` entirely).

**Interactivity:** a static page has no Caja runtime in the browser —
`main.caja` ran at build time and produced HTML — so a Caja event handler
(`onClick`) has nothing to attach it to and is dropped, with a warning at
build time. Use the JavaScript handlers instead:

```caja
import * from "@caja/js"

primaryButton("Run it")
	|> onClick([
		jsSetText(jsGetElementById("out"), jsStr("clicked"))
	])
```

`@caja/js` builds JavaScript the way `@caja/ui` builds HTML — a tree,
rendered to source at the end. It is correct by construction, and (unlike a
hand-written string) it can depend on values your Caja program computed, so
a page list or a label can cross into JavaScript as escaped, inert data.

For JavaScript you would rather just write, the `js` builtin parses a literal
at compile time and `jsRawStmt` drops it into a program:

```caja
import js

onClick([jsRawStmt(js.raw("alert('saved')"))])
```

There is one `onX` per event (`onInput`, `onSubmit`, ...),
and `scriptBlock` takes the same statement list for JavaScript that is not one
element's handler. The home page's "Interactivity" section is a working
example.

**Styles:** siriguela emits inline styles for anything a single element can
carry. Everything selector-based — a hover state, a pseudo-element, a media
query, an element or attribute selector — needs a real stylesheet, and the
theme owns it: `main.caja` writes `siteStylesheet()` to `dist/style.css` and
that is the whole of the CSS in this project. There is no hand-written CSS
string anywhere.

To add rules of your own, @caja/ui's CSS-in-Caja APIs are all available here:
`rule(...)` for element/combinator/attribute selectors, `media(...)` for
responsive rules, `class(...)` + `variant(...)` for a class with pseudo-states,
`keyframes(...)` for an animation, and `stylesheet(frames, rules, classes,
queries)` to render the lot. They are built by the same modifier chain that
styles a View, so a misspelled property is still a compile-time error.

## Dependencies

`package.json` declares both UI packages, and `caja init` runs your package
manager's install for you. **Neither is published to npm yet**, though, so
that install currently fails with a warning — until they are, link them
manually into a `node_modules/` at or above this folder:

```
node_modules/@caja/siriguela   ->  the theme
node_modules/@caja/ui          ->  the structural layer it is built on
node_modules/@caja/js          ->  the JavaScript builder @caja/ui uses
```

Both are real, direct dependencies: this project imports @caja/ui itself, not
only through the theme. siriguela does **not** re-export ui — every name it
exports is its own component — which is why a page writes `docSection(...)`
rather than assembling a `section` by hand, and why `Page`/`definePage` come
from the import below it rather than from the theme.

## Development

```
caja serve
```

Builds the project and serves `dist/` over HTTP for local development.

## Building

```
caja build
```

Compiles `main.caja`, runs it once to (re)generate `dist/`, copies `assets/`
into `dist/assets/`, and prints where the output landed.

## Deploying

`dist/` is a plain directory of static files — point any static file host
(Netlify, GitHub Pages, S3 + CloudFront, nginx, ...) at it.
